  <section class="prose" aria-labelledby="success">
    <h2 id="success">Where students go</h2>

    <p>Previous students have gone into a United States Navy fleet band, the Chautauqua summer instrumental program, and a touring position with Tim Zimmerman and the King&rsquo;s Brass. Others have gone on to study at the Eastman School of Music, Rice, Carnegie Mellon, and DePaul. An earlier student won a merit-based music scholarship to Harvard.</p>

    <h2>In lessons</h2>

    <p>We start from the sound you want and work back to the breath, the embouchure, and the motion that produce it. Most problems that feel technical turn out to be problems of concept first.</p>

    <p>I would rather you understand why a musical choice works than copy mine. You should leave a lesson able to hear what is going wrong on your own, with a plan for the week instead of a list of corrections.</p>

    <p>Students arrive wanting different things from their playing, and no two of them need the same preparation. An orchestral audition, graduate school, a military band, a teaching job, chamber music, a freelance career &mdash; a studio that only prepares you for one of those is preparing most of its students for something they will never do.</p>

    <h2>Beyond the universities</h2>

    <p>I teach privately, and I have taught at the Academy of Music at the University of Northwestern, the Greater Twin Cities Youth Symphonies, the Mannes Preparatory Division, Boston College High School, and at Chautauqua. Those students have ranged from grade school through adult, in lessons, sectionals, masterclasses, and chamber coachings.</p>
  </section>

